# Project Rules

## Project Shape
- This project is configured for SwarmForge with four AI-backed agents: specifier, coder, reviewer, and architect. Their CLI backends are configured in `swarmforge/swarmforge.conf`.
- Project languages: {{LANGUAGES}}.
- Before changing code, inspect the repository to identify its build, test, formatting, and dependency commands. Use tools already available in the project or on the local machine.

## Local-Only Tooling
- SwarmForge's own tooling (gherkin parser, gherkin mutator, gherkin IR DRY checker, swarm scripts, swarm-init, prompts) is self-contained. Do not clone, download, install, or update those from a remote repository or package registry.
- Third-party language analysis tools and project dependencies (pytest, coverage, radon, pylint, mutmut, jscpd, npm/pip packages, etc.) may be installed when a task requires them and the user grants permission. Before installing, state the exact install command and ask the user. Do not auto-install.
- Prefer installing into the project's local environment (project venv, pyproject.toml, package.json) when one exists. Ask which tool manager to use if it is unclear.
- If the user declines, stop and report the missing tool instead of skipping the check silently.

## Local Configuration
- Preserve project-local SwarmForge configuration under `swarmforge/`.
- Keep swarm state local under `.swarmforge/`, worktrees under `.worktrees/`, and shared scripts under `swarmforge/scripts/`.

## Agent Cycle
The swarm runs as one cycle per feature, not as a chain of small round trips.

```
specifier --one plan feature--> coder
coder --every change--> reviewer
reviewer --verdict fail--> coder               (rework; escalates to the architect at the round cap)
reviewer --verdict pass--> architect            (always)
architect --adjustments--> coder                (returns via reviewer)
architect --architecture sound--> specifier     (feature complete)
specifier: merge into working branch, NO commit/push, wait for user review
```

- The specifier sends only to the coder.
- The coder sends only to the reviewer, whoever sent it the work.
- The reviewer sends to the coder for rework, and forward to the architect, always, when the review passes.
- The architect sends adjustments to the coder and the completion signal to the specifier.
- Every role may send work to the coder. Only the specifier closes a feature.
- A handoff reaching the specifier from the architect means the feature is finished. The swarm itself (code, not an agent) publishes the final commit as a pull request for the user to review, detects the merge, syncs the worktrees and dispatches the next feature. The swarm never merges a pull request and never pushes to the base branch; the user does the merge.

## Specifications
- A requested module is split by the specifier into sequential features recorded in the feature plan (`.swarmforge/plan/`): `plan.json`, `general.md` and one `features/<id>.md` per feature. Each feature is a vertical slice of approximately 400 lines of production code; the figure is a guide, never a limit.
- Each feature has its own specification slice and, when the project uses Gherkin, its own Gherkin file. Features are handed to the coder strictly one at a time; the next starts only after the previous one is merged.
- Size and scope checks are advisory. They never reject a handoff, never send work back to the specifier and never cut the coder's work short.

## Handoffs
- Prefer terse, explicit handoffs that report state and request role-appropriate review. Do not include verifications or sender process narrative.

## Ownership
- Do not change another role's prompt or workflow ownership without explicit user direction.
